    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ante-trumbic"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ante-trumbic.jpg" alt="Ante Trumbić" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ante-trumbic">Ante Trumbić</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Ante_Trumbi%C4%87.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Arthur W. Page, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The idea of a common state of the South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> grew in the nineteenth century among Croat and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serb</a> intellectuals, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> made it possible. The Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>, which had fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> since 1914 and was occupied in 1915, sought to unite the Serbs and their kin, while South Slav politicians from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> lands, organised in exile as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslav-committee">Yugoslav Committee</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ante-trumbic">Ante Trumbić</a>, wanted a union of equals. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corfu-declaration">Corfu Declaration</a> of July 1917 the Serbian prime minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikola-pasic">Nikola Pašić</a> and Trumbić agreed on a single kingdom under the Serbian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karadordevic-dynasty">Karađorđević dynasty</a>. As Austria-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> collapsed in October 1918, a National Council in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagreb">Zagreb</a> proclaimed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/state-of-slovenes-croats-and-serbs">State of Slovenes, Croats and Serbs</a>, which, threatened by Italian advances along the Adriatic, sought rapid union with Serbia. In November an assembly at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/podgorica">Podgorica</a> deposed King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikola-i-of-montenegro">Nicholas of Montenegro</a> and voted for union with Serbia, a decision that led to armed resistance by his supporters. On 1 December 1918 the Prince Regent Alexander proclaimed the Kingdom of Serbs, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenes">Slovenes</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>, with his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter I</a> as king. The borders were settled in the peace treaties of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-saint-germain-en-laye-1919">Saint-Germain</a>, Neuilly and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-trianon">Trianon</a> and, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-rapallo-1920">Treaty of Rapallo</a> of 1920, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carinthia">Carinthia</a> voted to stay with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/bor-serbia"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bor-serbia.jpg" alt="Bor, Serbia" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/bor-serbia">Bor, Serbia</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Borizvazduha.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bor030.net, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The kingdom united some twelve million people in 1921, rising to about sixteen million by 1941, from regions with very different pasts: former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenia">Slovenia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vojvodina">Vojvodina</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia-herzegovina">Bosnia-Herzegovina</a>, and the formerly Ottoman lands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>, Montenegro, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosovo">Kosovo</a> and Macedonia. Nearly half the population was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a>, about two-fifths Catholic and about a tenth Muslim, and there were large German, Hungarian and Albanian minorities, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonians-ethnic-group">Macedonians</a> were officially treated as southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>. Around three-quarters of the people lived from farming, mostly on small plots. The agrarian reform begun in 1919 abolished the remaining serf-like <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenancy">tenancies</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonia-region">Macedonia</a> and redistributed large estates, but productivity stayed low and rural overpopulation severe; in 1921 about half the adult population was illiterate. Industry, banking and the best schools were concentrated in Slovenia, Croatia and Vojvodina, while mining, including the French-owned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> mine at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bor-serbia">Bor</a>, attracted foreign capital. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-depression">Great Depression</a> collapsed grain prices and pushed many peasants into debt, and in the later 1930s trade shifted heavily towards Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagreb">Zagreb</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ljubljana">Ljubljana</a> nonetheless grew as modern cities, and writers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivo-andric">Ivo Andrić</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miroslav-krleza">Miroslav Krleža</a> and the sculptor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-mestrovic">Ivan Meštrović</a> were active in the period.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/macedonian-front"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/macedonian-front.jpg" alt="Macedonian front" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/macedonian-front">Macedonian front</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Salonika_Army_Christmas_Card_1916_displaying_soldiers_of_all_Allied_nations.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown authorUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-yugoslav-army">Royal Yugoslav Army</a> was built on the Serbian army that had retreated through Albania in 1915 and returned in triumph after the breakthrough on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonian-front">Salonika front</a> in September 1918, and its officer corps remained overwhelmingly Serbian, which alienated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenes">Slovenes</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conscription">Conscription</a> filled the ranks, and the gendarmerie and army were used against internal unrest, including the Montenegrin rising of 1919 and Albanian kaçak rebels in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosovo">Kosovo</a> in the early 1920s. The kingdom maintained a small navy on the Adriatic and an air force equipped with French, British, German and domestically built aircraft, but the army as a whole remained poorly mechanised and dependent on horses and oxen. Foreign policy relied on alliances rather than strength: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/little-entente">Little Entente</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> from 1920–1921 guarded against Hungarian revisionism, a treaty of friendship with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> followed in 1927, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkan-pact">Balkan Entente</a> of 1934 linked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, Romania and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>. In April 1941 the army, only partly mobilised and strung out along long frontiers, collapsed within eleven days under attack from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. Several hundred thousand soldiers became prisoners, many of them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> held in Germany until 1945.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/nikola-pasic"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nikola-pasic.jpg" alt="Nikola Pašić" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/nikola-pasic">Nikola Pašić</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NikolaPasic--balkancockpitpol00pric_0191.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">unknown, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The kingdom was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a>, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vidovdan-constitution">Vidovdan Constitution</a>, adopted on 28 June 1921 by a narrow majority while Croat deputies boycotted the assembly, created a centralised state run from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> and divided it into 33 administrative districts that ignored historic regions. Serbian parties, above all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikola-pasic">Nikola Pašić</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-radical-party">People’s Radical Party</a>, dominated governments, the army and the civil service. The Communist Party, the third-largest party in the 1920 elections, was banned in 1920–1921. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatian-peasant-party">Croatian Peasant Party</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stjepan-radic">Stjepan Radić</a> demanded autonomy or a federal republic; Radić was imprisoned, then briefly joined the government in 1925. On 20 June 1928 the Radical deputy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punisa-racic">Puniša Račić</a> shot Radić and others in parliament, and Radić died that August. On 6 January 1929 King Alexander suspended the constitution and banned national parties, renamed the country the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> in October and replaced the districts with nine banovinas, most of them named after rivers. The constitution he imposed in 1931 kept royal power dominant. After Alexander’s assassination in 1934 a regency led by his cousin Prince Paul ruled for the young Peter II. Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan-stojadinovic">Milan Stojadinović</a> governed from 1935 to 1939, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cvetkovic-macek-agreement">Cvetković-Maček Agreement</a> of August 1939 created an autonomous Banovina of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/jasenovac-concentration-camp"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jasenovac-concentration-camp.jpg" alt="Jasenovac concentration camp" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/jasenovac-concentration-camp">Jasenovac concentration camp</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jasenovac_prisoners_enter_the_camp.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown authorUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> was the first state to unite most South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> and established the framework of Yugoslavia that, in a very different communist form, would last until 1991. It brought together <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenes">Slovenes</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosniaks">Bosniaks</a>, Montenegrins and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonians-ethnic-group">Macedonians</a> under one government for the first time, and many of its borders became the internal and external borders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josip-broz-tito">Tito</a>’s federation and of today’s successor states. Its central problem, whether the state should be a centralised kingdom dominated by Serbs or a federation of equal nations, shaped all later Yugoslav politics. The failure to answer it fed radical movements: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ustase">Ustaše</a>, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ante-pavelic">Ante Pavelić</a> after 1929, sought Croatian independence through terrorism and, with the Macedonian revolutionary organisation, organised the assassination of King Alexander in Marseille in 1934, in which the French foreign minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-barthou">Louis Barthou</a> also died. In 1941 the Ustaše state and the occupation regimes turned these divisions into mass murder of Serbs, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romani-people">Roma</a>, notably at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jasenovac-concentration-camp">Jasenovac</a> camp, and civil war between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chetniks">Chetniks</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslav-partisans">Partisans</a>. The interwar kingdom also produced an active cultural life, including the future Nobel laureate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivo-andric">Ivo Andrić</a>, who served as its diplomat, and the monumental work of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-mestrovic">Ivan Meštrović</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/dragisa-cvetkovic"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dragisa-cvetkovic.jpg" alt="Dragiša Cvetković" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/dragisa-cvetkovic">Dragiša Cvetković</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dragi%C5%A1a_Cvetkovi%C4%87_(1).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown authorUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>By 1941 the kingdom was surrounded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> states and their allies. Under heavy German pressure, the government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dragisa-cvetkovic">Dragiša Cvetković</a> signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tripartite-pact">Tripartite Pact</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> on 25 March 1941. Two days later, on 27 March, air force officers led by General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dusan-simovic">Dušan Simović</a> overthrew the regency in a coup welcomed by crowds in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>, and the seventeen-year-old Peter II was declared of age. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a> ordered the destruction of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>, and on 6 April 1941 German aircraft bombed Belgrade, killing thousands, while German, Italian and Hungarian forces invaded. Croatian units in particular showed little will to fight, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ustase">Ustaše</a> proclaimed the Independent State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagreb">Zagreb</a> on 10 April, and the army signed an unconditional surrender on 17 April. King Peter II and the government fled into exile, eventually to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>. The country was partitioned: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> annexed northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenia">Slovenia</a> and occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ljubljana">Ljubljana</a> and much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a> and controlled Montenegro and, through Albania, most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosovo">Kosovo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/backa">Bačka</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baranja">Baranja</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> took most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonia-region">Macedonia</a>, and the Independent State of Croatia received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia-herzegovina">Bosnia-Herzegovina</a>. The government-in-exile backed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/draza-mihailovic">Draža Mihailović</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chetniks">Chetniks</a>, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josip-broz-tito">Tito</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslav-partisans">Partisans</a> won the war, and a constituent assembly abolished the monarchy on 29 November 1945.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> was the first state to unite most South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a>, created in 1918 from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>, Montenegro and former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> lands, but unresolved conflict between Serbian centralism and Croatian demands for autonomy left it fragile and it collapsed within days of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> invasion in April 1941.</em></p>

